00:22:50.890 --> 00:22:55.530
I have this section where I'm really trying to figure out Pam and Roy's relationship.

564
00:22:56.230 --> 00:22:58.070
And so I wrote this prompt.

565
00:22:58.090 --> 00:22:59.670
What was your biggest fight?

566
00:22:59.910 --> 00:23:05.050
I wrote, our biggest fight ever was the time that I told him that if I found out that he

567
00:23:05.050 --> 00:23:10.410
committed a murder that I would turn him into the police, like if they didn't know who did

568
00:23:10.410 --> 00:23:12.810
it and he wasn't planning to turn himself in.

569
00:23:12.990 --> 00:23:17.270
He got really, really mad and said that families are supposed to protect each other.

570
00:23:17.410 --> 00:23:20.110
And I said, yeah, but not from murder.

571
00:23:20.330 --> 00:23:25.310
He said, what if he was really, really sorry and I could tell that he would never murder

572
00:23:25.310 --> 00:23:26.010
someone again?

573
00:23:26.730 --> 00:23:27.530
I don't know.

574
00:23:27.710 --> 00:23:29.710
I still think I'd probably turn him in.

575
00:23:30.010 --> 00:23:30.970
That's amazing.

576
00:23:31.510 --> 00:23:31.850
Yeah.

577
00:23:32.070 --> 00:23:32.830
That's amazing.

578
00:23:32.830 --> 00:23:38.030
And that it's such like one of those hypothetical bullshit things that you get in a fight about

579
00:23:38.030 --> 00:23:40.770
because the real things aren't working.

580
00:23:41.030 --> 00:23:41.390
Yeah.

581
00:23:41.550 --> 00:23:45.890
So you don't talk about the real things and then you have all of this pent up stuff about

582
00:23:45.890 --> 00:23:46.530
a hypothetical.

583
00:23:46.910 --> 00:23:54.190
Well, I think it kind of touched on a type of disconnect in their like fundamental values,

584
00:23:54.790 --> 00:23:55.530
you know?

585
00:23:55.770 --> 00:23:55.990
Yeah.

586
00:23:55.990 --> 00:24:03.850
Like Roy values family loyalty over everything and Pam sort of is a rule follower and feels

587
00:24:03.850 --> 00:24:10.550
like she has a sort of moral obligation to the world that sometimes could supersede the

588
00:24:10.550 --> 00:24:10.910
family.

589
00:24:11.170 --> 00:24:11.390
Yes.

590
00:24:11.510 --> 00:24:11.710
Yeah.

591
00:24:11.910 --> 00:24:13.530
I was like, that's really interesting.

592
00:24:13.970 --> 00:24:20.010
I also wrote, I used to be an illustrator, but Roy told me it was time for us to get regular

593
00:24:20.010 --> 00:24:20.530
jobs.

594
00:24:20.530 --> 00:24:25.510
I used to just be a receptionist on the side while I drew and tried to get work as an illustrator.

595
00:24:26.090 --> 00:24:30.430
I'm artistic, but I've given it up to have a more stable life and job.

596
00:24:30.590 --> 00:24:31.890
That is great.

597
00:24:32.010 --> 00:24:37.650
Because for me, that fills in that question of like, why is Pam there?

598
00:24:37.890 --> 00:24:39.350
Why is she the receptionist?

599
00:24:39.470 --> 00:24:44.230
Why does she seem so beat down in this job on top of everything else?

600
00:24:44.230 --> 00:24:49.870
It seems like a real step back for her just being there.

601
00:24:50.490 --> 00:24:50.730
Yeah.

602
00:24:51.030 --> 00:24:57.250
I felt like I needed to answer those questions for myself when I started the job.

603
00:24:57.950 --> 00:25:01.250
Theater students out there, how about that?

604
00:25:01.370 --> 00:25:05.790
Background work, building your character, things people don't ever see.

605
00:25:06.330 --> 00:25:07.270
It's true.

606
00:25:07.690 --> 00:25:12.810
Well, thank you very much to Cartuna for asking us to dig around in our personal photos.

607
00:25:12.810 --> 00:25:15.590
Because now I have my Pam document back.

608
00:25:15.610 --> 00:25:16.030
That's right.

